-- T1: the encoder's output does not depend on its own history --
   Sequential, one thread. The learned width survives a reset by design, so
   this asks whether what survives can be seen on the wire. Needs two shapes.

   12 pairs checked on two arms.

-- T2 and T3: the same work, run in sequence and then together --
   4 threads x 6 rounds x 4 shapes, every encode memcmp'd against the
   reference. T2 joins each thread before starting the next, so the two
   differ only by overlap: a defect that shows in both is not a concurrency
   defect, and that distinction is why 12.5 asks for both.

  T2 threads in sequence                   96 encodes   native 0  ffi 0  ffi-hosttc 0  roundtrip 0  spurious-err 0
  T3 threads together                      96 encodes   native 0  ffi 0  ffi-hosttc 0  roundtrip 0  spurious-err 0

